        <body>
            <div xml:id="d136">

                <pb facs="0286" n="158" sameAs="frus:frus1928v01/pg158"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.2312Anti-War/6</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Peruvian Ambassador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Velarde</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1928-08-27"><hi rendition="#italic">August 27,
                        1928</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: The position taken by that [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">the</hi>] Department, in having initiated the
                    celebration of a multilateral treaty for the renouncing of <pb facs="0287"
                        n="159" xml:id="pg159"/> war in the world, signed to-day in Paris by the
                    nations which have accepted it thus marking a glorious stage in the history of
                    humanity, will be, for all time, a legitimate source of pride to the great
                    nation of the United States of America.</p>
                <p>In fulfillment of the express instructions of the President, Mr. Augusto B.
                    Leguia, and of his Government, I hasten to express to Your Excellency that Peru
                    formally, and with sincere enthusiasm, adheres to the anti-war pact
                    above-mentioned and that as soon as a certified copy thereof shall have been
                    received, it will be submitted to the cognizance of the National Congress for
                    ratification.</p>
                <p>Peru, who has ever been a partizan of cordiality and peace in international
                    relations and who aspires, therein, to nothing more than to the predominance and
                    triumph of justice cannot remain indifferent to the position taken which the
                    signing of the pact above referred-to implies for the progress of mankind.</p>
                <p>I would therefore request Your Excellency to be so good as to consider Peru and
                    its Government as adhering to the transcendental treaty in question.</p>
                <p>Accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hernán Velarde</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
